# D27 accept-both, SELF-HOST resolver + runtime lanes: the canonical `^` move
# sigil immediately AFTER a FUNCTION TYPE whose return type is a PRIMITIVE
# (`int(int) ^cb`).
#
# AXIS CELL: glyph = `^` · position = named param whose type is a function
# type · function-type RETURN kind = PRIMITIVE (the `named` sibling cell is
# `d27_sh_caret_fntype_spec_named.gg`) · lanes = Rust gg (C + LLVM),
# self-host resolver, self-host runtime.
#
# WHY THIS IS ITS OWN CELL: `Type(params)` is parsed SPECULATIVELY — the
# parser commits to "this is a function type" only when the token that
# FOLLOWS the parameter list is one of an accept-set. The `^` glyph was
# missing from that accept-set, so the speculation BACKTRACKED and the
# construct was re-parsed as something else. The primitive-return and
# named-return speculation sites are two DISTINCT predicates in the parser,
# hence two fixtures.
#
# RED-verified against the PRE-port self-host lane: this program failed to
# compile through the self-host driver (the C compile step exited non-zero)
# while its `!` twin built and ran, printing `6`. Post-port the self-host
# lane compiles it and its output MATCHES Rust gg.
#
# No new `!` site is added for the control (`fmt_no_new_move_bang_in_migrated_corpora`
# in tests/lints.rs is shrink-only and at zero headroom); the corpus has no
# postfix-`!` twin of this exact position, so the durable guard for the
# glyph PAIR at this predicate is the `sh_parser_caret_predicate_siblings`
# lint in tests/lints.rs, which asserts every ported predicate names BOTH
# tokens.
#
# Expected stdout: 6

int double_it(int x):
    return x * 2

void apply(int(int) ^cb):
    print(cb(3))

void main():
    apply(^double_it)
